    blurb: 'The anatomy-and-physiology lab animal, above all for the muscles: an adult mammal, with dozens of named muscles on each side.',
    about: 'The cat is the classic specimen of the second-year anatomy and physiology course because it is an adult mammal built on the same plan as a human, with every muscle large enough to separate with fingers and a probe. Students spend weeks on the muscles before opening the body. The cat also shows what a running and climbing predator needs: a free-floating collarbone, a long spring of a back, retractile claws, and several muscles that people do not have at all. Supply companies say most lab cats come from animal shelters where they had already been put down; many colleges also offer a model or a virtual dissection to any student who asks.',
    cls: [['Phylum', 'Chordata'], ['Class', 'Mammalia'], ['Order', 'Carnivora'], ['Family', 'Felidae'], ['Genus', 'Felis']],
    orient: 'On its back, limbs tied out with string passed under the tray, head to your left. The cat’s right side is nearest you. For the back and the outer limbs, turn it over.',
    tools: 'Large dissecting tray, string, scalpel, blunt probe, forceps, scissors, bone cutters, gloves, and a plastic bag to keep the cat moist between labs.',
    care: 'The skin comes away with almost no cutting: lift it and push the connective tissue apart with your fingers. Separate muscles along their natural seams of white fascia before you cut anything, and when you must cut one, cut across its middle (bisect it) so both ends can be named. Wrap the cat in damp paper towels and seal it in its bag after every lab.',
    q: 'cat dissection anatomy muscles', photoQ: 'cat dissection anatomy',
